  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday Night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1020 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nQuiet and pleasant fall weather will persist through the middle of\nnext week as mid-level ridging dominates the central CONUS and surface\nhigh pressure slides eastward toward the Lower Mississippi and \nOhio Valleys. Southerly return flow will become more established \nTuesday and Wednesday, promoting a steady warming trend. Limited\nfire weather concerns may occur Tuesday and Wednesday as \nsoutherly winds crank up and become gusty, with gusts in excess of\n25 mph at times, due to a developing trough/surface low that will\npass north of the area Wednesday into Thursday. Afternoon highs \nwill climb into the low-to-mid 60s on Tuesday and reach the mid- \nto-upper 60s to near 70 degrees on Wednesday under mostly sunny \nskies, with overnight lows moderating from the 30s on Monday night\ninto the 50s by Wednesday night.\n\nBy Thursday into Friday, guidance depicts a progressive upper-\nlevel trough amplifying across the western CONUS, moving over the\nRockies by Friday afternoon. Lee cyclogenesis will occur at the \nsurface as the trough begins to move over the Plains, causing a \ndeepening area of low pressure that will drag a cold front behind \nit as the surface low lifts northeast Friday evening into late \nFriday night. While notable spread in guidance remains regarding \nexact evolution and track of the trough and associated frontal \ntiming, the NBM introduces widespread low-to-medium rain chances \n(20-40%) along and ahead of the approaching cold front late Friday\nnight through Saturday night, with the front moving through the \nCWA sometime Saturday afternoon/evening. \n\nBehind the cold front, cooler and drier continental air is \nfavored to filter back into eastern Oklahoma and northwest \nArkansas for next Sunday, returning daytime temperatures to the \nlow-to-mid 60s and overnight lows into the 30s and 40s.\n\nMejia",
